# 5. Core Components Deep Dive

# A. Music Upload Pipeline

Artists/labels upload music.

Flow:

```text
Upload Service
    |
Transcoding Service
    |
Generate multiple bitrates
(64kbps, 128kbps, 320kbps)
    |
Store in Object Storage
(S3/GCS/HDFS)
    |
Push to CDN
```

## Why multiple bitrates?

Adaptive streaming.

Poor network:

* Use lower bitrate

Good network:

* Use higher bitrate

Interview follow-up:

> “How does Spotify reduce buffering?”

Answer:

* Adaptive bitrate streaming
* CDN edge caching
* Prefetching next chunks
* Chunk-based streaming

---

# B. Streaming Architecture

This is the most important section.

## Songs Are Chunked

Instead of sending full songs:

```text
Song -> small chunks (5–10 sec)
```

Benefits:

* Fast startup
* Adaptive bitrate
* Seek support
* Retry smaller segments

Protocols:

* HLS
* MPEG-DASH

---

## Why CDN is Critical

Popular songs are cached close to users.

```text
User -> Nearby CDN Edge -> Music chunk
```

Without CDN:

* Origin overload
* High latency
* Massive bandwidth costs

Good line in interview:

> “Spotify is fundamentally a CDN-heavy architecture.”

---

# C. Metadata Storage

Song metadata:

* song_id
* artist
* album
* genre
* duration

Needs fast lookup.

Typically:

* Relational DB for consistency
* Read replicas for scaling

Possible:

* MySQL
* PostgreSQL

---

# D. Playlist Service

Playlists are:

* Read heavy
* Append heavy

Schema:

```text
Playlist
PlaylistSongs
```

Optimization:

* Denormalized playlist cache

Storage:

* Cassandra/DynamoDB works well

Why NoSQL?

Because:

* Massive scale
* High write throughput
* Flexible schema

---

# E. Search System

Spotify-like search supports:

* Typo tolerance
* Prefix search
* Fast autocomplete

Use:

* Elasticsearch

Indexed fields:

* song name
* artist
* album
* lyrics

Follow-up:

> “Why not SQL LIKE queries?”

Answer:

* Poor scalability
* Slow fuzzy matching
* Not optimized for inverted indexing

